--- replicactl.py
+++ replicactl.r5-production.restorefix.py
@@ -60,6 +60,14 @@
   - The record verify-<scratch>.json is created exclusively, and an existing one is refused. Updates go through a fresh,
     exclusively created, random-named temp that replaces the record only while the record is still this verification's
     own file. Only our own temp is ever removed.
+  restore-timeout fix (restore-timeout-diagnosis, after integration run a7e53855b0f9f535):
+  - PostgreSQL is PID 1 of its container (no --init), so it reaps every process orphaned inside the container, and
+    a reaped child that died from a signal is a crashed "untracked child process" to it: every session is ended and
+    the server reinitializes. Plain `timeout -s KILL` (GNU) also KILLs its own process group, itself included, so
+    the killed pg_restore was orphaned to the postmaster and the restore bound crash-restarted the whole server.
+  - the restore now runs as `exec timeout --foreground -s KILL`: timeout KILLs pg_restore only, stays alive and
+    reaps it itself (exit 137). The container's timeout must be GNU coreutils (BusyBox has no --foreground and
+    always leaves an orphaned watcher); otherwise nothing is created.
 
 Nothing here activates anything by itself: every mutating command needs an explicit --confirm flag naming the
 action, and production profiles additionally refuse to run unless the node's ALLOW file exists (the same pattern as
